---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ConnectsStrip from '../components/ConnectsStrip.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import RepetitiveGraphic from '../components/RepetitiveGraphic.astro';
import ServiceCards from '../components/ServiceCards.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import StickyCta from '../components/StickyCta.astro';
import { cta, ctaHref, nav, footerColumns } from '../content/site-v1';

/**
 * ⚠️ THE RETIRED HOMEPAGE. This was `/` from the Style C redesign on 29 Aug
 * 2026 until 1 Sep 2026, when the owner swapped in the wireframe design. It is
 * kept live at /v1, noindex, because the owner asked for the old homepage to
 * stay available for reference — not because anything still links to it.
 *
 * Three things hold while it is here, and all three go together if it is ever
 * deleted:
 *
 *  1. `noindex` below, so it cannot compete with `/` in search.
 *  2. `Disallow: /v1` in robots.txt.ts.
 *  3. The sitemap filter in astro.config.mjs.
 *
 * Its nav, CTA and footer come from `site-v1.ts`, not `site.ts`, so the page
 * stays a faithful snapshot — see the header of that file. `Hero.astro` and
 * `StickyCta.astro` import from there for the same reason, and both are used
 * by this page and nothing else.
 *
 * ⚠️ Do not "fix" this page. It is a record. If something here looks stale
 * against `/`, that is the point.
 */
---

<Base
  noindex
  title="Homepage, previous design · Thread"
  description="The previous Thread homepage, kept for reference."
>
  {/* businessSchema is deliberately NOT set. The entity is stated once, on the
      page it belongs to, and that is `/` — two URLs claiming the same
      ProfessionalService is exactly the ambiguity the flag exists to avoid. */}
  <Header navItems={nav} cta={cta} ctaHref={ctaHref} home="/v1#top" />
  <Hero />
  {/* Full-bleed band rather than a section, so it sits outside .wrap and
      supplies its own rules top and bottom. On the ink ground since the
      Style C redesign, which makes it the page's first hard horizontal and
      separates the hero from the argument that follows. */}
  <ConnectsStrip />

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
      §01 · The busywork

      Anchors changed with the redesign, 29 Aug 2026. The page used to run
      #services / #how / #ownership over four sections; it now runs
      #busywork / #build / #contact over three. #contact is unchanged and
      must stay so: the hero CTA, the footer and vercel.json's /systems-map
      301 all target it. The three retired fragments now land at the top of
      the page, which is the harmless failure mode for a fragment.

      No top rule: the connects strip above draws its own on both edges, and
      a third here would double them. --------------------------------- */}
  <Section id="busywork" noRule>
    {/* Two siblings of the grid rather than nested blocks, which is
        load-bearing: flat, the DOM order is already the phone order, and the
        desktop arrangement is explicit grid placement instead.

        ⚠️ Cut 29 Aug 2026 on the owner's instruction: the lead-in "Maybe your
        team is:" and the closing block ("These little inefficiencies add up."
        / "That's the busywork I build out of your business."). The section is
        now the heading and the drawing. `.bw-close` was the only thing in row
        two of the grid, so the drawing no longer spans two rows and the
        `align-self:end` that dropped the closer level with the end of the list
        went with it. The copy is in git if it comes back — recover it rather
        than rewriting it. */}
    <div class="bw-grid">
      <div class="bw-intro">
        <h2 class="bw-head">
          If it happens over and over, it should be <span class="hl">automated.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <RepetitiveGraphic />
    </div>

    {/* The ask sits after the drawing: by this point a visitor has watched
        their own week build up, multiply and get ticked off. The seven
        descriptions are still in the page, in the drawing's hidden list. */}
    <div class="btn-row bw-cta">
      <a href={ctaHref} class="btn">{cta}</a>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
      §02 · What I build

      The one section on the quiet ground. Carries the five build types.

      ⚠️ BuildGraphic.astro is finished and NOT rendered, on the owner's
      instruction 29 Aug 2026 ("remove the graphic completely from the process
      section for now"). The component, its two compositions and its keyframes
      are kept rather than deleted because "for now" is explicit — putting it
      back is this import and this one line.

      That does leave a component in src/ that renders nowhere, which is
      against the standing rule CLAUDE.md records from the 18 Aug sweep. It is
      deliberate and it is the owner's call to make it permanent either way.
      --------------------------------------------- */}
  <Section id="build" wash>
    <h2 class="bd-head">Your process. Just without all the work.</h2>
    <p class="lead bd-lead">
      I look at how the work actually gets done, find the repetitive steps,
      bottlenecks, and workarounds, then build a simpler system around them.
    </p>
    <p class="mono-note bd-note">That might be a:</p>

    <ServiceCards />
  </Section>

  {/* §03 renders its own marker: it sits inside the left column of the
      contact grid rather than running the full width, so it cannot come from
      Section.astro. */}
  <ContactSection />
  <Footer columns={footerColumns} />
  {/* Phone only, and the last thing in the document so it is last in the
      tab order too — it is a shortcut, not the primary path. */}
  <StickyCta />
</Base>

<style>
  /* ---------- §01 ----------
     35/65. The drawing is the substance here and the heading is the frame,
     which is the reverse of every other section on the page. */
  .bw-grid {
    display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,35fr) minmax(0,65fr);
    column-gap:64px; row-gap:48px;
  }
  /* Explicit placement, because the DOM order is the phone order and the
     desktop arrangement is the exception. One row now that the closing block
     is gone; the heading sits at the top of the narrow column beside it. */
  .bw-intro { grid-column:1; grid-row:1; }
  /* ⚠️ Inside :global() in full. `.repetitive-graphic` is
     RepetitiveGraphic.astro's root element, so a scoped selector compiles to
     `.repetitive-graphic[data-astro-cid-THIS]` and silently matches nothing —
     landmine 18, which already cost §01's accent rule once under the ocean
     design. */
  :global(.bw-grid > .repetitive-graphic) { grid-column:2; grid-row:1; }
  .bw-head { margin:0; }
  .bw-cta { margin-top:44px; }

  /* The highlight behind "automated." A background gradient rather than a
     border or a pseudo-element, so it wraps with the text if the line ever
     breaks mid-phrase. 62% is the baseline: below that the wash cuts the
     descenders, above it the phrase looks underlined rather than marked. */
  .hl {
    background:linear-gradient(transparent 62%, var(--color-accent-soft) 62%);
  }

  /* ---------- §02 ---------- */
  .bd-head { margin:0 0 18px; max-width:26ch; }
  .bd-lead { max-width:58ch; }
  .bd-note { margin-top:18px; }

  /* ---------- responsive ----------
     820px is where the hero grid stacks and both drawings swap to their narrow
     compositions, so §01 stacks at the same width and the whole page changes
     shape once rather than three times. */
  @media (max-width:820px) {
    /* One column, and the placements reset to auto so the DOM order takes
       over: heading, lead, list, then the closing statement. That order is
       the whole reason the three blocks are flat siblings — see the note in
       the markup. */
    .bw-grid { grid-template-columns:1fr; row-gap:34px; }
    .bw-intro { grid-column:auto; grid-row:auto; }
    :global(.bw-grid > .repetitive-graphic) { grid-column:auto; grid-row:auto; }
  }

  /* ⚠️ §01's ask is hidden on phones for the same reason the hero's is:
     StickyCta carries the same label, pinned, from the moment the page loads,
     so this one sits a few hundred pixels above a permanent copy of itself.

     Both this and Hero's rule are coupled to StickyCta existing and pointing
     at the same place. If the sticky bar goes or changes, both come back in
     the same commit — otherwise the page has no in-flow CTA at all between
     the headline and the form. */
  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .bw-cta { display:none; }
  }
</style>
